00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:04.309 align:start position:0%
welcome to the presentation of our work  titled diversity policy gradient for  sample efficient quality diversity  optimization in this presentation you  will learn about our algorithm called  qtpg which is at the frontier between  two family of algorithms namely quality  diversity and policy gradient methods  this work was done in collaboration with  the imperial college instadeep and  sorbonne university  first let me introduce some key concepts  of this paper  reinforcement learning is the problem  faced by an agent that must learn a  behavior through trial and error  interactions with an environment to  maximize obtained rewards  deep reinforcement learning and  especially policy gradient methods have  proven to be efficient to train large  neural controllers in continuous control  settings  they benefit from high sample efficiency  compared to evolutionary methods  this advantage is due to the fact that  they leverage information at the time  step level policy gradient methods  exploit the structure of the reward  function and also exploit the analytical  structure of the controller which allows  the sample complexity of these methods  to be independent of the parameter space  dimensionality  a major drawback of these methods is  their limited exploration mechanisms  that usually focus on exploring the  action space making the resolution of  highly deceptive problems very difficult  if not impossible  we will see what is a deceptive problem  in a minute  quality diversity is a new family of  optimization algorithms that aims at  searching for a collection of solutions  that are diverse and high performing at  the same time the objective is to cover  the range of possible solution types and  to return the best solution for each  type  quality diversity methods usually build  on black box optimization methods such  as evolutionary algorithms and have the  advantage of producing multiple working  alternatives when a single solution fail  additionally their explicit exploration  mechanisms make them very well suited to  tackle hard exploration problems  on the other hand they are usually  sample inefficient when compared to  reinforcement learning methods and  struggle to train large neural networks  that are needed in continuous control  tasks  in this work we will focus on three  environments that all exhibit strong  deceptiveness  point maze on the left of your screen is  a simple environment in which an agent  must find the exit of the maze the  reward function is the negative distance  between the agent and the exit of the  maze making it particularly difficult  for standard optimization methods that  do not extensively explore falling into  the local optimum of running into the  wall  at maze in the middle is a difficult  continuous control environment in which  an articulated ant robot needs to learn  to walk before finding the exit of the  maze  as in point maze this environment  present a strong local optimum  the last environment called antrap  rewards its agent proportionally to its  forward velocity as in point maze and  ant maze the set of walls in front of  the agent at the start of an episode  makes it very difficult for standard  optimization techniques such as policy  gradient methods to overpass the local  optimum  in this work we introduce the idea of a  diversity policy gradient that drives  solutions towards more diversity  the diversity policy gradient can be  used in combination with the standard  policy gradient to produce high  performing and diverse solutions  our algorithm called qdpg builds on map  elites and replace random diversity  search with diversity gradient updates  a full qdpg iteration consists of  solution selection in the map elites  grid then solution mutation which is  done half according to classical policy  gradient and half according to diversity  gradient  solution are then evaluated and added  back into the map elites grid if they  are novel or high performing  all encountered transitions are stored  into a replay buffer that is used to  train a diversity critic and a quality  critic from which gradients are derived  in qdpg diversity is thus brought by the  policy gradient updates and by the  structural pressure for diversity from  the map elites grid  to derive the diversity policy gradient  we first compute a diversity reward  similar to the standard reward provided  by the environment the diversity reward  is computed for each visited state based  on the distance between the considered  state and all previously visited states  by any solution evaluated so far the  idea is that a solution visiting novel  states that have not been seen so far  should be rewarded for its novelty  maximizing the diversity rewards in the  same manner as we maximize for the  standard rewards through the use of  diversity policy gradient updates will  induce an increase of diversity in the  population of solutions  a drawback from using the diversity  gradient is the need for defining state  descriptors that are used to compare  states and get a sense of proximity  between one state and another  here you can see qdpg in action on the  bottom row of the screen  our algorithm manages to solve all three  environments finding the exit on both  mazes and running fast in add trap by  first avoiding the walls  as a comparison the soft actor critic  agent which is a state-of-the-art  reinforcement learning algorithm does  not overcome any of the local optima in  all three environments  we compared qdpg with ablations and  policy gradients baselines in all  environments  results can be seen on the left of your  screen  even reinforcement learning agents that  implement intrinsic motivation fail to  solve ant maze and ant trap  we also compared qdpg to its  evolutionary competitors in ant maze  the ratio column compares the sample  efficiency of qdpg to that of  competitors  as you can see our algorithm is orders  of magnitude more sample efficient than  compared methods  to the best of our knowledge this work  is the first to introduce a diversity  gradient in combination to the  structural pressure from map elites to  implement divergent search  we believe that an interesting further  step would be to combine our diversity  component with genetic mutations to have  robust exploration in a wider range of  situations  thank you for listening to our  presentation
 

